---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "LeafFilter Reviews 2026: Cost & Alternatives";
const description =
  "An independent LeafFilter review — how the micromesh performs, what it really costs per foot, the warranty fine print, and six alternatives compared.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "raptor-stainless-micromesh",
    "name": "Raptor Stainless Steel Micro-Mesh",
    "category": "DIY Micromesh, Stainless",
    "badge": "Best DIY Alternative",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 5200,
    "pros": [
      "Genuine 316 stainless micromesh at roughly a fifth the installed cost of LeafFilter",
      "Attaches to the gutter lip without going under shingles, so no roof warranty concerns",
      "Rigid V-bend design resists the sagging that ruins cheap micromesh",
      "Cuts with tin snips and installs with a screw gun — a realistic weekend project on one story",
      "Blocks pine needles and shingle grit as effectively as professionally installed micromesh"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "You are on the ladder — not viable or safe on a steep or two-story roof",
      "Product warranty only; no installation warranty and nothing transferable"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For an accessible single-story roofline, this delivers most of what LeafFilter does for hundreds rather than thousands. The trade is your labor and your safety.",
    "description": "Raptor is the product we point people toward when they have a reachable roofline and a functioning sense of self-preservation about ladders. It uses genuine 316 stainless micromesh — the same working principle as LeafFilter, blocking pine needles and shingle grit that pass straight through screen guards — at a materials cost that typically lands under $1,000 for an entire average house, against $3,600-7,000 installed for the professional option. The V-bend rigidity is what separates it from the flimsy micromesh sold at big box stores: cheap mesh sags within two seasons, and sagging mesh pools water and mats debris, converting a filter into a clog. Raptor holds its shape. Installation attaches to the front gutter lip and rests on the back, never sliding under the shingles, which avoids the roof warranty complications that some systems create. In practice, an average single-story home takes a competent DIYer a weekend with tin snips and a screw gun. The honest limits are safety and coverage. On a two-story house or a steep pitch, this is not a project you should take on, and that is precisely the case where paying for professional installation is buying something real. You also get a product warranty only — nothing covering workmanship, and nothing transferable to a buyer."
  },
  {
    "id": "gutterglove-pro",
    "name": "Gutterglove Pro",
    "category": "Professional Micromesh",
    "badge": "Best Professional Alternative",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 2100,
    "pros": [
      "Same micromesh principle as LeafFilter with an aluminum frame and stainless mesh",
      "Installed through local contractors, so pricing is competitive and quotes are comparable",
      "Typically $12-22 per linear foot installed — well below LeafFilter's usual range",
      "Available in a DIY version if you want the same product without the installation",
      "Long track record; the platform many regional micromesh installers build on"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Warranty terms vary by installing contractor rather than being nationally standardized",
      "Local installer quality is the variable — vet the contractor, not just the product"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most direct like-for-like alternative. Same technology, usually meaningfully less money, with contractor quality as the variable you have to manage.",
    "description": "Gutterglove Pro is the answer for homeowners who want professional micromesh installation without the national-brand premium. The technology is fundamentally the same as LeafFilter — fine stainless mesh on a rigid frame, blocking needles and grit while surface tension carries water through — and in terms of what ends up on your roof, the performance difference is small. What differs is the business model. Gutterglove is installed by independent local contractors, which means you get comparable competitive quotes rather than a single negotiated in-home price, and installed costs typically land in the $12-22 per foot range against LeafFilter's usual mid-$20s and up. On an average 200-foot house that is commonly a $1,500-3,000 difference for a functionally similar outcome. The flip side is that quality control is distributed. LeafFilter's warranty comes from one national company with one standard; Gutterglove's installation warranty comes from whichever local contractor you hire, and their reputation, insurance, and longevity are yours to verify. Ask how long the installer has been in business, get proof of insurance, and confirm the warranty terms in writing. There is also a DIY version of the same product if your roofline is accessible and you would rather do the work yourself."
  },
  {
    "id": "leafguard-one-piece",
    "name": "LeafGuard One-Piece System",
    "category": "Reverse Curve, Integrated Gutter",
    "badge": "Best for Heavy Rainfall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 1800,
    "pros": [
      "One-piece design replaces gutter and guard together, eliminating seams that leak",
      "Reverse curve handles heavy downpour volume better than micromesh in our assessment",
      "Larger downspouts move more water than standard residential sizing",
      "Professionally installed with a national warranty",
      "No separate gutter condition to worry about — everything is new"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Reverse curve designs allow small debris like pine needles and shingle grit to enter",
      "Cost is comparable to or higher than LeafFilter since you are replacing gutters too"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Better than micromesh in torrential rain, worse with fine debris. The right pick for heavy-rain regions without pine trees.",
    "description": "LeafGuard takes a fundamentally different approach: rather than adding a guard to your existing gutter, it replaces the entire gutter with a one-piece seamless unit whose front edge curves back over the opening. Water follows the curve by adhesion and enters through a slot underneath; leaves ride over the edge and fall to the ground. The practical advantage over micromesh is downpour capacity. Where micromesh can be overwhelmed in torrential rain — water arriving faster than it can pass through fine mesh, sheeting over the front — the reverse curve slot handles large volume well, and LeafGuard pairs it with oversized downspouts that move more water than standard residential sizing. Eliminating seams is a genuine secondary benefit, since seams are where conventional gutters eventually leak. The trade-off is exactly inverted from micromesh: the entry slot is large enough to admit small debris. Pine needles, shingle grit, and seed pods can and do get inside, where they are harder to remove than from an open gutter. If you have pines, this is the wrong system. If you are in a heavy-rainfall region with broad-leaf trees, it may genuinely outperform micromesh. Cost is comparable to LeafFilter or higher, because you are buying new gutters as part of the package."
  },
  {
    "id": "hometec-micromesh-diy",
    "name": "HomeTec Stainless Micromesh Panels",
    "category": "DIY Micromesh, Budget",
    "badge": "Best Budget DIY",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 3600,
    "pros": [
      "Lowest cost per foot of any genuine stainless micromesh we would recommend",
      "Panels snap into place quickly, making a large house a realistic weekend job",
      "Effective against pine needles and small debris, the main reason to choose micromesh",
      "No under-shingle installation, so no roof warranty complications",
      "Easy to remove and reinstall if you need to service the gutter"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Thinner frame than premium DIY options, so it needs support spacing followed carefully",
      "Fit is less precise on older or irregular gutters, requiring more trimming"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to get real micromesh performance. Follow the support spacing carefully and it holds up; ignore it and it sags.",
    "description": "HomeTec panels are the budget entry point into genuine stainless micromesh, and they earn a place here because the mesh itself is real — this is not the perforated aluminum sold as micromesh at some retailers. Against pine needles, seed pods, and shingle grit, it performs like micromesh should, which is the entire reason to choose this category over cheaper screens. Installation is fast: panels snap in place along the gutter, and an average house is a genuine weekend project rather than a multi-weekend ordeal. Removal is equally easy, which is worth more than it sounds if you ever need to get into the gutter to clear a downspout. The compromise relative to premium DIY micromesh is frame rigidity. The frame is thinner, which means the manufacturer's support spacing has to be followed precisely rather than approximately — get it wrong and the mesh develops the sag that pools water and mats debris, undoing the benefit. Fit is also less forgiving on older gutters with irregular profiles or on homes with unusual gutter sizes, so budget time for trimming. For a homeowner with a straightforward single-story roofline, standard gutters, and a willingness to read the installation instructions carefully, it delivers the core benefit at the lowest price here."
  },
  {
    "id": "amerimax-lock-in-screen",
    "name": "Amerimax Lock-In Gutter Screen",
    "category": "Perforated Screen, Aluminum",
    "badge": "Best for Broad-Leaf Trees Only",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 9400,
    "pros": [
      "Extremely inexpensive — often under $2 per linear foot",
      "Installs in minutes per section with no fasteners on most gutter profiles",
      "Genuinely effective against leaves, twigs, and larger debris",
      "Aluminum construction will not rust or degrade in sunlight",
      "Easy to lift out for occasional cleaning without tools"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Pine needles and shingle grit pass straight through — useless in evergreen areas",
      "Can pop loose in high wind or under heavy snow load if not screwed down"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right answer only if your debris is exclusively large leaves. If you have a single pine, buy micromesh instead.",
    "description": "There is a real case for spending $300 instead of $5,000, and it comes down to what actually falls on your roof. If your yard is maples and oaks with no evergreens, a perforated aluminum screen blocks essentially all of the debris that would otherwise clog your gutters, and does it for under $2 per linear foot. The Amerimax lock-in screens install in minutes per section without fasteners on most standard gutter profiles, lift out easily for the occasional rinse, and will not rust or break down in UV. For that specific situation, spending twenty times more on micromesh buys very little additional protection. The situation where this is the wrong purchase is equally clear. Pine needles are thin enough to drop straight through the perforations, and shingle grit washes through unimpeded, so in an evergreen area or with an aging asphalt roof this product does close to nothing — and worse, it makes the gutter harder to clean because you have to remove the screens first. The other practical weakness is retention: lock-in screens can pop loose under high wind or heavy snow load unless you add a screw per section, which is worth doing at installation rather than after they blow into the yard. Buy it for the right yard and it is outstanding value; buy it for the wrong one and you have wasted a weekend."
  },
  {
    "id": "gutterbrush-insert",
    "name": "GutterBrush Simple Gutter Guard",
    "category": "Brush Insert",
    "badge": "Easiest Install",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.1,
    "reviewCount": 6800,
    "pros": [
      "Simply drops into the gutter — the fastest installation of anything here, no tools at all",
      "Works with irregular, odd-sized, or damaged gutters where rigid guards will not fit",
      "Very inexpensive and completely reversible if you change your mind",
      "Keeps large debris suspended above the gutter floor so water still flows underneath",
      "A reasonable rental-property or stopgap solution while you plan something permanent"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Debris collects in the bristles and eventually requires pulling and cleaning the brushes",
      "Does nothing for pine needles or fine grit, which settle straight through"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A genuine stopgap, not a solution. Useful for odd gutters or as a temporary measure, but it converts gutter cleaning into brush cleaning.",
    "description": "GutterBrush is the least sophisticated product here and occasionally the most practical one. It is a large cylindrical bristle brush that drops into the gutter channel, with no tools, no fasteners, and no fitting required. Large debris lands on top of the bristles rather than settling into the gutter floor, so water continues to flow underneath while leaves sit above it. Because it conforms to whatever it is dropped into, it works on irregular, oversized, half-round, or damaged gutters where every rigid guard system would need custom fabrication. It is also completely reversible: if you decide against it, you pull it out and you are exactly where you started. Where it falls down is the medium term. Debris does not disappear — it accumulates in the bristles, and after a season or two in a leafy area you are pulling brushes out, shaking them clean, and putting them back, which is meaningfully less pleasant than it sounds and not obviously better than cleaning the gutter. Fine debris like pine needles and shingle grit falls straight through the bristles and settles in the gutter as if nothing were there. Treat this as a stopgap for an odd gutter profile or a rental property, not as an alternative to micromesh for a house you intend to keep."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Reviews",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "LeafFilter Reviews",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/leaf-filter-reviews/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is LeafFilter worth the money?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on your roof. For a two-story or steeply pitched home where DIY installation is genuinely unsafe, and where you want a single company accountable under a transferable lifetime warranty, it is a defensible purchase. For an accessible single-story roofline, high-quality DIY micromesh delivers comparable debris protection for $500-1,200 in materials versus $3,000-7,000 installed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does LeafFilter cost per foot?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "LeafFilter does not publish pricing, but homeowner-reported quotes generally run $17-45 per linear foot installed, clustering in the mid-to-high $20s. That puts an average 200-foot home at roughly $3,600-7,000. Comparable professionally installed micromesh from regional contractors typically runs $12-22 per foot.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does LeafFilter actually work?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The micromesh genuinely blocks pine needles, seed pods, and shingle grit that pass through screen-type guards, and that is a real advantage. Its limitations are inherent to all micromesh: water can sheet over the mesh in torrential rain, debris still accumulates on top of the guard, and fine organic material can glaze the mesh over years in damp, shaded conditions.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you still have to clean gutters with LeafFilter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You will rarely need to clean inside the gutter, but debris still piles on top of the guard and in shaded damp areas can mat down and restrict flow. Most owners find an occasional pass with a leaf blower is still needed. No gutter guard from any manufacturer is genuinely maintenance-free.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the LeafFilter warranty?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A transferable lifetime warranty covering the product and workmanship, promising that if the system fails to keep gutters clear the company will return to clean and repair at no cost. Before signing, verify in writing how claims are initiated, the response-time commitment, what storm and ice damage exclusions apply, and whether the transfer to a new owner requires a fee or registration window.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good alternative to LeafFilter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For DIY on an accessible roofline, Raptor Stainless Micro-Mesh delivers comparable filtration for a fraction of the cost. For professional installation, Gutterglove Pro uses the same micromesh principle at typically $12-22 per foot through local contractors. In heavy-rain regions without pine trees, LeafGuard's reverse-curve system handles downpour volume better.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is the LeafFilter sales appointment so long?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "LeafFilter sells exclusively through in-home appointments that commonly run 90 minutes to three hours and often end with a same-day discount. The practical response is to decide before the appointment that you will not sign that day, ask for the quote in dollars per linear foot with total footage stated, and compare it against two other micromesh installers.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "leaffilter reviews",
      "leaf filter reviews",
      "leaffilter cost",
      "gutter guards",
      "micromesh gutter guard",
      "home improvement"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Independent Review
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">LeafFilter</span> Reviews: Is It Worth the Price?
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We looked at the product itself, the pricing model, the warranty terms, and what six competitors offer for the same money. Here is an independent take, with no affiliation to LeafFilter.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#verdict"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            Skip to the Verdict
          </a>
          <a
            href="#alternatives"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See Alternatives
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="24.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What It Is</a>
          <a href="#performance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Real-World Performance</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Cost Breakdown</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Warranty Fine Print</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Alternatives Compared</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Verdict</a>
          <a href="#buying-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Choosing a Guard</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          LeafFilter is the most heavily advertised gutter guard in the country, which means most homeowners encounter it through a sales appointment rather than research. The product itself is genuinely good — a fine stainless micromesh that does what it claims. The harder questions are what it costs relative to competitors, what the lifetime warranty actually covers, and whether the same protection is available for half the price. This review answers all three.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: What LeafFilter Actually Is -->
        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What LeafFilter Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            LeafFilter is a surgical-grade stainless steel micromesh gutter guard mounted on a rigid uPVC frame. The mesh has openings measured in microns rather than millimeters, fine enough to block not just leaves but pine needles, shingle grit, and seed pods — the debris that defeats most cheaper guards. It installs at a slight pitch over the existing gutter, secured to the gutter lip rather than under the shingles, which matters for roof warranty reasons.
            </p>

          <p>
            The company sells exclusively through in-home sales appointments with professional installation. You cannot buy LeafFilter at a home center or install it yourself, and pricing is not published — it is quoted at the appointment based on linear footage, roof pitch, story height, and gutter condition.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">LeafFilter By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">275</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Micron mesh opening size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$17-45</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical cost per linear foot, installed</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$3-8k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical whole-house project total</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">Lifetime</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Transferable warranty, with conditions</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Micromesh Guards Work</h3>
          <p>
            Water clings to surfaces through surface tension, which lets it pass through an extremely fine mesh while solid debris slides off the top. That is the whole principle, and it works — micromesh is genuinely the most effective gutter guard category, far better than screens, foam inserts, brushes, or reverse-curve helmets at handling small debris like pine needles and roof grit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limitation of every micromesh guard, LeafFilter included, is heavy rainfall. In a downpour, water arrives faster than surface tension can pull it through fine mesh, and some sheets over the edge. Every micromesh manufacturer deals with this; the better ones minimize it through mesh area, pitch, and frame design.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: How LeafFilter Performs in Practice -->
        <div id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How LeafFilter Performs in Practice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Setting aside the sales process, the product deserves a fair assessment on its own merits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Does Well</h3>
          <p>
            The micromesh genuinely stops fine debris. Pine needles, which slip through nearly every screen-type guard and are the number one cause of clogged gutters in much of the country, do not pass through 275-micron mesh. Shingle grit — the granules that wash off asphalt roofs and silt up gutters over years — is likewise blocked. On a house surrounded by pines or with an aging roof, this is a real, meaningful difference from cheaper guards.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rigid uPVC frame is also better engineered than most competitors' frames. It holds the mesh taut and flat, which matters because sagging mesh pools water and collects debris. Installation attaching to the gutter lip rather than sliding under shingles avoids the roof warranty complications that reverse-curve systems can create.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where It Falls Short</h3>
          <p>
            Three limitations show up consistently in owner accounts and are inherent to the design rather than defects:
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Overshoot in heavy rain.</strong> In intense downpours, water can sheet over the mesh and past the gutter, particularly on steep roofs or in valleys where runoff concentrates. This is physics common to all micromesh, not a LeafFilter flaw, but it is worth knowing before you assume gutters will handle everything afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Debris still accumulates on top.</strong> Micromesh keeps debris out of the gutter, not off the roof. Leaves and needles pile on top of the guard and either blow off or, in damp shaded areas, mat down and slowly restrict flow. Many owners find they still need an occasional visit with a leaf blower — less often than cleaning gutters, but not never.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pollen and shingle silt can glaze the mesh.</strong> Over several years, fine organic material can partially seal the mesh surface in shaded, damp conditions, reducing throughput. Rinsing restores it, but that means a ladder.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
