          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Choose Any Gutter Guard
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whether you buy LeafFilter or something else, the same criteria decide whether you are happy in five years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Guard to Your Trees</h3>
          <p>
            Pines, hemlocks, and spruces mean you need micromesh, full stop — needles pass through anything coarser. Broad-leaf trees like maples and oaks can be handled by a good perforated screen at a fraction of the cost. Take an honest inventory of what actually falls on your roof before choosing a category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Attachment Method</h3>
          <p>
            Guards that slide under the first course of shingles can void some roof warranties and can lift shingles over time. Systems that attach to the gutter lip or fascia avoid that entirely. Ask specifically, and if the answer is "under the shingles," check your roof warranty terms first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look at Frame Rigidity, Not Just Mesh</h3>
          <p>
            Cheap micromesh with a flimsy frame sags within a couple of seasons, and sagging mesh pools water and traps debris — turning a good filter medium into a bad gutter guard. Frame quality is what separates the $9-per-foot DIY products from the $2.50 ones, and it is where the money is well spent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fix the Gutters First</h3>
          <p>
            Guards installed over sagging, undersized, or improperly pitched gutters inherit every one of those problems and make them harder to see. If your gutters overflow at the seams, pitch backward, or are pulling away from the fascia, address that before adding guards, not after.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Get Three Quotes, in Dollars per Foot</h3>
          <p>
            Total-project quotes are impossible to compare because footage varies with how it is measured. Insist on price per linear foot and the footage count. The spread between installers for identical micromesh work is routinely 40% or more.
          </p>
        </div>

